Z||000016||Voytek||12-17-2001||03:21 PM||||I think yo-sweet made a generalization, BUT, I don't think Japanese cars are that great, either.<P>I had a neighbor with a Honda Prelude (about 1990 - 1992).  What a POS!  I had a friend with an Eagle Talon (Mitsubishi Eclipse).  THE WORST POS I have EVER seen!  Two engines and two trannies within 5 years.<P>I've owned two Toyotas - an '81 and '82.  Funny enough, a friend had the same tranny problems (mainly 3rd gear) in his 80's Celica that my '81 SR5 Tercel had.  Also, with religious maintenance, my '82 started smoking badly at around 200,000 KKM.  Not that this is low mileage, but I always hear how Japanese engines go 'forever'.<P>True, Japanese cars USED to have much better quality than American vehicles, but a recent Automotive survey (heard it on the radio about 4 months ago) said that the quality gap between American and Japanese automobiles has been bridged in the last few years.<P>BTW, a Japanese car made in the US is still a Japanese car.  Just like a Toyota made in Canada is still a Toyota and a Korean Daewoo made in Poland is still Korean.  One can't blame the workers for faulty engineering.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||MinnGreenGT||12-17-2001||03:30 PM||||So where does that leave the Cavalier?  Hmm... marketed here as the "Chevrolet" Cavalier here, and across the Pacific (japan) it is the "Toyota" Cavalier.  Same body/drivetrain, just different nametag?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I agree- a Japanese design built in the US or anywhere else is still a Japanese design.<P>I don't think that "all imports are crap" either- especially not based on one review (think "Fieros... they catch fire").  But I personally prefer a US built car.  No Biggie.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
